<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.15.2: Linear Function Constant Rate of Change</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01517</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.15.2: Linear Function Constant Rate of Change</md:title>
    <md:uuid>8dc2a960-e54b-4340-add6-ed0fee67e548</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>Here is a graph of \(y=f(x)\) where \(f(x)=2x+5\).</para>
<image alt="Graph of an increasing linear function, x y plane, origin O." src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e22c1ae4cc52cd736d0fa6fa34869c1f9ff0e9d9"/><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> How do the values of \(f\) change whenever \(x\) increases by 1, for instance, when it increases from 1 to 2, or from 19 to 20? Be prepared to explain or show how you know. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The value of \(f\) increases by 2 whenever the input increases by 1. For example: </para>
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
      <item> From the graph, we can see the \(y\)-value increasing by 2 when the \(x\)-value increases by 1. </item>
      <item> \(f(1)=7\) and \(f(2)=9\). The difference between \(f(1)\) and \(f(2)\) is 2. Likewise, \(f(20)=45\) and \(f(19)=43\), and \(f(20)−f(19)=2\). </item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Here is an expression we can use to find the difference in the values of \(f\) when the input changes from \(x\) to \(x+1\). </para>
    <blockquote>
      <para>\(\lbrack 2(x+1)+5 \rbrack − \lbrack 2x+5 \rbrack \)</para>
      <para>Does this expression have the same value as what you found in the previous questions? Show your reasoning.</para>
    </blockquote>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Yes. \(2(x+1)=2x+2\). Replacing \(2(x+1)\) with \(2x+2\) and performing arithmetic gives \(f(x+1)−f(x)=2\). </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> How do the values of \(f\) change whenever \(x\) increases by 4? Explain or show how you know. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> The values of \(f\) change by 8 if the input is increased by 4. Increasing the input by 4 is the same as increasing the input by 1 four successive times, and each time the value of \(f\) increased by 2. </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Write an expression that shows the change in the values of \(f\) when the input value changes from \(x\) to \(x+4\). </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> \(f(x+4)−f(x)\) can be expressed as \(\lbrack 2(x+4)+5 \rbrack− \lbrack 2x+5 \rbrack \). </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Show or explain how that expression has a value of 8. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Applying the distributive property to \( \lbrack 2(x+4)+5 \rbrack − \lbrack 2x+5 \rbrack \) gives us: \(2x+8+5−2x−5\) or \(2x−2x+8+5−5\), which equals 8. </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
</section>
